      "title": "Great British Railways sets course for rail freight growth",
      "summary": "Press release Great British Railways sets course for rail freight growth Government sets a new rail freight target to help businesses move more goods by rail, strengthen supply chains and support economic growth. From: Department for Transport and The Rt Hon Heidi Alexander MP Published: 8 September 2026 government sets new target to increase rail freight by at least 40% by 2040, putting the industry on track for 75% growth by 2050 Great British Railways will be tasked with growing rail freight by record amounts to back British businesses moving more goods and supporting economic growth meeting the target could mean around £15 billion more goods moved by rail each year Today (8 September 2026), the government has unveiled its vision for Great British Railways to back British businesses, strengthen supply chains and drive economic growth, with a new target to increase rail freight by at least 40% by 2040. The target is the first milestone towards the government’s ambition of increasing rail freight by at least 75% by 2050, giving industry the confidence to invest while ensuring Great British Railways plays a central role in delivering growth across the country. Rail freight already carries goods worth almost £34 billion each year to the UK’s economy. Meeting the new target would increase this figure by around £15 billion, helping businesses move more products across Britain, strengthening supply chains and supporting growth. A single freight train can carry enough materials to build around 30 homes. Network Rail estimates the new targets could increase rail freight of construction materials by 45% and boost the movement of goods like cars, wine, white goods and medicine by around two-thirds. Transport Secretary, Heidi Alexander, said: Great British Railways will have a c",
